WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.217
Captions are not decorative subtitles added at
the end.

2
00:00:03.217 --> 00:00:08.915
They carry the information contained in the
audio: spoken words, speaker identity when it

3
00:00:08.915 --> 00:00:10.543
matters, and meaningful sounds.

4
00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:14.540
A viewer should be able to follow the
instructional message without relying on

5
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.462
hearing the soundtrack.

6
00:00:15.578 --> 00:00:20.678
Automatic speech recognition can create a
useful first draft, but technical terms,

7
00:00:20.678 --> 00:00:23.653
names, punctuation, and timing often need
correction.

8
00:00:23.653 --> 00:00:29.384
Review captions against the final audio, keep
cues synchronized, and break lines at readable

9
00:00:29.384 --> 00:00:30.203
phrase boundaries.

10
00:00:30.203 --> 00:00:34.914
The reviewed caption file—not the raw machine
output—belongs with the release.

11
00:00:35.018 --> 00:00:39.910
Accessibility works best as a production
requirement, not an emergency repair.

12
00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:45.344
RIT recommends planning accessible media while
a course is designed and provides captioning

13
00:00:45.344 --> 00:00:47.016
options for Panopto content.

14
00:00:47.016 --> 00:00:52.667
Build review time into the schedule, keep a
corrected transcript, and verify that visuals

15
00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:56.299
also communicate their essential meaning
through narration or description.
